edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186512308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512743 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "27aac6e5-28a9-5d6f-bdc3-723dde44ae4d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512745 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512745 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512745 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512745 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186512184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512745 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "290c0fd4-796b-5b98-9efb-32925a5e1121" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512881 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512881 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334747" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512881 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512881 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a third-order numerical method?\n\nI. $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{4}+5(Δx)^{6}$\n\nII. $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{2}+3(Δx)^{3}$\n\nIII. $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+8(Δx)^{5}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512881 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "288ecca2-4a65-5e47-af70-06519e1d4ea1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512881 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{4}+5(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{4}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{2}+3(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $4(Δx)^{2}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+8(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{3}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512903 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512903 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334749" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512903 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512903 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a third-order numerical method?\n\nI. $τ(Δx)=3(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{6}$\n\nII. $τ(Δx)=0.5(Δx)^{4}+8(Δx)^{7}$\n\nIII. $τ(Δx)=7(Δx)^{5}+5(Δx)^{6}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512903 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "80296baf-83a9-58bd-9c2b-7e79ee3813aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512903 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=3(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $3(Δx)^{4}=3(Δx)^{3+1}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.5(Δx)^{4}+8(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.5(Δx)^{4}=0.5(Δx)^{3+1}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=7(Δx)^{5}+5(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $7(Δx)^{5}=7(Δx)^{4+1}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512904 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512904 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334826" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512904 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512904 #edb/ref 1071 "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $4.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.25$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.25)≈0.3125.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.10,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.10)≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512904 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "87976b3b-af1b-5fcb-8c01-533b581f6a90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512904 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.25$ to $0.10.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.25}{0.10}=2.5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $4,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{4}=(2.5)^{4}=39.0625.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.10,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.10)≈\\frac{0.3125}{39.0625}=0.008.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512951 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512951 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334642" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512951 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512951 #edb/ref 1071 "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.10,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.10)=0.24.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.05,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.05)=0.02.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512951 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ebc091be-da12-5892-8a06-6dc1bcb20797" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512951 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.10$ to $0.05.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.10}{0.05}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={2}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.24$ to $0.02.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.24}{0.02}=12.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p+1} & ≈12 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(12) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.585… \\\\ p & ≈2.585….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512962 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0a696d2d-bcf2-5d59-8cbe-c8b94fa6e1c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512962 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186512904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512963 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186513016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512963 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "10f1833a-b6bb-5d67-9812-3e3fe1c988ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512963 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186512881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512992 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186512962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512992 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "630e5c31-48d0-509b-b72b-9cf2dd9d0d36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512992 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186308683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513006 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186512963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513006 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "98e32d8b-896e-5b81-93c2-6516863ca64c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513006 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186512951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513016 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186512992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513016 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b87614ba-eda8-5587-9a21-7a8fbab941af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513016 